  "id": "COMPOUND:1628 - PROTEIN:6337",
  "verbalization": "Prasterone is shown to have a target relationship with multiple entities that are implicated in human biology, suggesting a potential role in human physiology. The evidence points to Prasterone interacting with the Emopamil-binding protein-like, which is a target of 2-[(4S)-4-hydroxy-3,6-dioxodioxan-4-yl]acetic acid and Homo sapiens, indicating a possible involvement in cellular processes. Similarly, Prasterone is linked to Cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6A2, mitochondrial, which is a target of Azaribine and also has a human target, suggesting a broader role in mitochondrial function. These findings collectively imply that Prasterone may influence human cellular processes through interactions with proteins involved in metabolic pathways and mitochondrial function. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Prasterone and Homo sapiens, though the exact biological pathways remain to be fully elucidated.",
